-- NIL, NOT A GREY BLOB. This fell back to rbxassetid://6031090998 -- Roblox's generic
-- placeholder -- for every name it did not have, and the UI library asks for more names
-- than this table holds: "chevron-down" on every dropdown, "layout-grid" on an empty
-- tab, "save"/"folder-open"/"trash-2" in its dialogs. So each of those drew the same
-- grey placeholder, and the dropdown chevron in particular was a grey square on every
-- dropdown row in the script.
--
-- The library's own contract is that a resolver may answer nothing: it checks the image
-- it got back and draws a text "›" arrow when there is none. Answering nil is what lets
-- it do that. An icon we do not have is now no icon, which reads as deliberate; a
-- placeholder reads as broken.
--
-- AN EMOJI IS AN ICON WE CAN ACTUALLY HAVE. The reason this table cannot grow is that
-- an asset id is whatever number the image was uploaded under -- it cannot be worked
-- out from the outside, and a guessed one renders as somebody else's picture. An emoji
-- has no id: it is text the font already carries. So the tab builder in the UI library
-- takes a short string that resolves to no image and draws it as a label in the icon
-- slot, tinted and animated exactly as an image icon is. Pass one straight to
-- Window:CreateTab -- it does not belong in this table, which is asset ids.
--
-- It must be NON-ASCII to be taken as an emoji, not merely short and unresolvable.
-- Without that test, CreateTab("Signs", "signpost", 56) -- a plain name this table
-- happens not to have -- drew the word "signpost" squeezed into a sixteen-pixel slot
-- instead of falling back to the honest blank. A name is ASCII; an emoji is not.
--
-- That is the way out "hammer" and "layers" took: both borrowed the construction
-- picture as stand-ins, and the owner asked for the polish -- Hammer and Blueprints
-- now pass a character straight to CreateTab (207 and 082), and the two stand-in
-- rows below are gone. A real picture for either means BOTH halves again: a row here
-- with the uploaded id, and the tab's CreateTab line going back to GetIcon("name").
local function GetIcon(name)
    return LucideIcons[name]
end

